  ],
  "text": "Code is a very long list of instructions for flipping switches, written in something a human can stand to read. The machine only understands numbers meaning move this, add that, jump there. Writing that directly is possible, and almost nobody does it, because it is unbearable. So we built a language slightly above it, and a program that translates downward. Then a language above that one. Then another above that. Each layer exists so the layer above it can stop caring about something. You write add these two numbers. Something below decides which piece of silicon does it. And under that, an operating system is deciding who gets memory, and when. None of the layers are optional, and none of them are visible from the top. Which is how three lines you can read end up moving billions of switches you cannot. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
